("v2584", "key", "text")
("v2585", "type", "String")
("v2585", "scalar", "Skip to content\nAaron Alderman\nAuthor questions\nThreads\nAtlas / Map\n\nQUESTIONS / AUTHOR\n\nWhat do I owe\nthe people who enter my stories?\n\nWords make worlds. Worlds teach us what to notice.\n\nWriting lets me imagine lives, choices and possibilities beyond my own. I want the worlds I make to invite attention: to people, to consequences, and to things a neat explanation can leave out.\n\nFollow the questions ↓\n\nA question to practise, not a finished answer.\n\nAUTHOR\n\nA story is a way of paying attention.\n\nAn invented world still makes choices about who matters, whose voice is heard, and what a reader is invited to hope for. I want to make those choices with care, while leaving room for surprise, ambiguity and a reader’s own response.\n\nWhere the question becomes concrete.\n\nPEOPLE\n\nWho gets to be a person in this story?\n\nA character should have room to want, refuse and surprise me. I want to notice when I have reduced someone to a lesson, a convenient obstacle or a reward for somebody else.\n\nExplore attention to others →\n\nCONSEQUENCES\n\nDoes the world remember what its characters do?\n\nChoices should leave traces. Worldwright explores storytelling in which a world can develop through connected situations and consequences, with its construction open to inspection.\n\nExplore Worldwright →\n\nCRAFT\n\nCan the same idea find its right form?\n\nA scene might want to become prose, a drawing, an animation or something a person can explore. Creator Studio is the developing workspace for bringing those forms into conversation.\n\nExplore Creator Studio →\n\nHONESTY\n\nWhat am I asking the reader to believe?\n\nI want to distinguish invention from a claim about the real world, and conviction from certainty. Revision is part of taking both the story and the reader seriously.\n\nExplore revision →\nAnother side of the same life.\n\nThese commitments meet in one life. Each brings a different question into focus.\n\nSourcerer →\nAuthor →\nDad →\nChristian →\n\nAaron Alderman\n\nA few things I’ve built. A few things I’m still trying to understand.\n\nGitHub ↗\nLinkedIn ↗\nBuilt with Worldwright.io ↗\nAssurance\nLicence ↗\nChristian stewardship covenant ↗")
